#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064038 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064167 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a4550c-7ed7-58aa-8928-23dd1cfd0cd7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Monomial"}] :question/id #uuid "368cbbae-4eff-5eff-a8f3-d3e8359447a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-364" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\,\\textrm d x$." :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=2$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int x^2 \\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^{2+1}}{2+1} + C = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}+C\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{x^{3}}{3} + C) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(C) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3}) + 0 &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 3x^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064039 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064168 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85af4ca3-5e66-522a-9e5a-6efb1c7114ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Radical Function"}] :question/id #uuid "818f4fbe-e251-57bd-90ae-b6517c6f2f89" :question/math-academy-id "e-367" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt x$." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the square root as a rational power,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt x= x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the power rule with\n\n$$\nn=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\sqrt{x}dx &= \\intx^{1/2}dx \\\\\n&= (x^{1/2 + 1})/((\\frac{1}{2} + 1)) + C \\\\\n&= (x^{3/2})/((\\frac{3}{2})) + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{2x^{3/2}}{3} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{2x^{3/2}}{3} + C) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{2x^{3/2}}{3}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(C) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3/2}) + 0 &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x^{3/2 - 1} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{1/2}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064040 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3174cae9-484e-5ca3-8e5c-3c9cddc86145" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Reciprocal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "c25e6037-3900-5314-b9a9-262fef07061c" :question/math-academy-id "e-368" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac 1 {x^3}$ and that $F'(x) = f(x)$, calculate $F(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if\n\n$$\nF'(x) = f(x)\n$$\n\nthen integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF^{′}(x) &= f(x) \\\\\n\\intF^{′}(x)dx &= \\intf(x)dx \\\\\nF(x) &= \\intf(x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {x^3}= x^{-3}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the power rule with $n=-3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(x) &= \\intf(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int\\frac{1}{x^{3}}dx \\\\\n&= \\intx^{-3}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{-3 + 1}}{-3 + 1} + C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{x^{-2}}{2} + C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2x^{2}} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064167 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a4550c-7ed7-58aa-8928-23dd1cfd0cd7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Monomial"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e6c66f6-6133-59f6-a79d-0cbf1615accf" :question/math-academy-id "q-113665" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=20$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{20+1}}{20+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{21}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{21})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{21}\\cdot 21{x}^{20} & = \\\\ {x}^{20}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234299 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3174cae9-484e-5ca3-8e5c-3c9cddc86145" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Reciprocal Function"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44529618-fcf7-5581-8bd3-a0df68de242f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71100" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-1/7}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/7+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{7}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/7}}{(\\frac{6}{7})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
